entry.summary:
littleFedi is "A small ActivityPub server written in Go" It was officially released (I think) yesterday, and is another project from Stefano Marinelli of BSSG fame. A few years ago I'd decided I wanted to "own my content" 🙄 so I ran an instance of Mastodon. Mastodon is overkill and I didn't enjoy it. Then, later, I installed an instance of Go2Social, which worked well. Trouble was, I was all-in with social media at the time and being on a single-user instance felt lonely...nothing useful in "local" or "trending". Discovery was difficult. It was just me and I didn't like it. Fast forward to today, and things have changed. I've deleted all of my Meta-related accounts and LinkedIn. I thought about quitting Mastodon as well, but FOMO always gets the better of me. Since I would rather not quit Mastodon completely, I figured I would try running a single-user instance again. Maybe the quiet would be good for me. I'd seen @Stefano's posts about a new ActivityPub server he was working on and the timing of the first release couldn't be better, so I installed it today at Jack Baty Social.
It's running on my Hetzner VPS behind Caddy as a proxy. I love Caddy. Here's the entire Caddy config for the domain: social.baty.net { encode gzip reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081 }
I've just started poking around, but littleFedi seems like it's got a lot going for it. At first I wanted to use my root domain for my handle (@jack@baty.net) but since I'd previously used that via Ghost, I ran into caching issues and didn't want to bother, so now I'm @jack@social.baty.net. The big question is whether to migrate followers there, or start fresh and maybe just announce the move from my main @jbaty@social.lol account. I think I'll give it a minute before deciding. littleFedi is a compelling new entry in the FediverseSpaceArena. ✍️ Reply by email

Intro
Quick, what does this match?
/^(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)(?:-((?:0|[1-9]\d*|\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*)(?:\.(?:0|[1-9]\d*|\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*))*))?(?:\+([0-9a-zA-Z-]+(?:\.[0-9a-zA-Z-]+)*))?$/;

// Take
// your
// time...
//
// ...still decoding?
//
// OK, keep reading :)


That's the official regexp from semver.org. It
validates version numbers like:
// matches
"1.2.3"
"0.10.0"
"2.0.0-rc.1"
"1.0.0-alpha.1+build.5"
"1.0.0+20260930"

// doesn't match
"01.2.3"   // leading zero
"1.2"      // missing patch
"v1.2.3"   // no "v" prefix allowed
"1.0.0-01" // numeric pre-release with a leading zero
"1.2.3-"   // empty pre-release


Don't get me wrong, I love regexps, but in practice you probably spend
a bunch of time writing one, testing it against some cases, and moving
